well an editor starts on day two of  shooting sometimes you start a little  bit before in terms of discussing the  script but mostly I'm there to look at  the dailies the day after they shoot  things and work very closely with the  director to check that he's getting what  he wants and that everything is working  not just on a technical level but maybe  to do with performance and the structure  of the story and you know shooting is a  bit like a shopping expedition you're  trying to get everything crammed into  your trolley before you know the money  runs out and you start filming and you  have to kind of have your wits about you  to know that you've got everything you  need to be able to tell the story as  well as you can and then you're seeing  the raw state of the dailies and you're  the first person to see those which is a  great thrill or if you previous day was  a nightmare for everybody it could be  disastrous but that's that that's a very  exciting moment to the beginning of the  day so the assistants will go through  and they'll they'll take the the sound  of the picture sync them up and then  assemble the the raw dailies into a  fashion that the editor has dictated it  works for them the assistants prepare it  for me in a manner that is very  particular to me that they all know I'll  go through in a very methodical way and  review the footage and my goal it's to  inhabit that footage as deeply as I can  so that I can understand what the  director is going after I try not to  read the script too much because I don't  want to be influenced too much by the  script because sometimes they'll shoot  something that doesn't quite match what  the script is and you can try forever to  make it feel like it's matching the  script or you can look at the footage as  the footage itself there's actually an  old saying that you write one script you  shoot another and you edit another and  so I feel at the end of beating that I'm  at the end of that process I'm really  trying to tell a story in the way that  everyone wanted it to be told but in the  way that it's been told by the by  responding to the footage and not  responding to the original intention if  that makes sense as soon as I finish the  scene the first thing I do is I bring in  my assistants and ask them what they  think because it's really important to  me I just I'd like to have very very  smart people around me people who  challenge me and challenge my opinion  every director is different when  I work on the fast and furious movies  like Justin Lin doesn't like to sit  still for too long so he's like there  were two to three editors at all times  so that he can go in one room watch a  sequence give notes and then go in  another room watch a sequence and give  notes quentin is incredibly hands-on he  wants to be there for every part of the  process I did a cut of Django and  assembly while the movie was shooting  but when we came back in like that  assembly was really simply to  familiarize myself with the material and  and try some things that could be  interesting when when I did it with  Quentin like he really likes to go back  and start from scratch and then so I  build it all up then I show the director  and then the director sort of says right  you've done everything wrong and then  they build it all up that how they want  it and then the producers say that's not  working at all and then they build it  the way they want it and then the  writers come in and say well that's not  the original tension whatsoever so then  you rebuild it again and then it gets  back to basically what you did in the  first place people come in and it's  lovely that they have that objectivity  and it can be white actually quite magic  because you know the director will come  in and see something that they didn't  intend but it's something that they  really love that you've done and all of  a sudden they're like oh that's really  cool well why don't we try this and all  of a sudden you're off in a different  direction  it's an incredibly creative job so first  and foremost you've got to be very  responsive to not only you know picture  and editing and but sound and music and  performance and it covers you know your  in the still point of a turning world  there's many many things coming in from  you from all sorts of departments and  you know you're right in the middle with  the director and you have to kind of I  suppose you have to have good personal  skills in terms of working under great  pressure if there are fundamental  problems with a piece on a script level  those are that are pretty undeniable  those are always difficult to to sort of  reconcile and there are any number of  ways to try to deal with that  it depends on resources it depends on a  lot of different things personalities  sometimes if you're in a challenging  situation you know either  director-producer or if you're in  television a network executive maybe a  bit reactive those can be particularly  challenging situations it's interesting  sometimes I'll go for an interview and I  won't get the job and I don't mind if I  don't get the job because I'll meet the  editor that directors ended up working  with and realize that they the editor is  a very different person than me I can't  talk about sports to save my life and if  the director wants to talk about sports  as they're editing I'm not gonna be the  person they want to hang out with for 10  hours a day in a very small room in the  dark and I think that's okay and so I  think as an editor you've got to be  someone who is fun to be around or at  least good to talk to you and is a  creative collaborator but also someone  who it's kind of like a bartender or a  psychiatrist because at the end of the  day you're you know just sort of talking  to someone as you wipe the Barragan so  how was your day a good friend of mine  says you have to be a part of edit or  part therapist and I think there's a lot  of truth to that more is expected of the  editor we have had to become full-on  sound designers and oftentimes music  editors as well you know now that we're  editing in hi-def we can project that  and it looks pretty good when you were  working on a flatbed with film like it  wasn't even something that was possible  you would have to do a temp mix whenever  you were into a screening now I mean the  the the my tracks in the avid this is  what we screened when we put it in front  of an audience so I have to make sure  that the stuff sounds as good as it  possibly can you start off you're  walking into an empty room day one and  you don't have any pens or pencils or  equipment and invariably twelve months  later you need to have brought  everything in create something walk away  and then leave that room exactly as you  found it you know the other skills are  patience you know it's a long haul I  mean some people are on a film for a day  and others are on for a long time and  we're in the latter category I mean I  to save on 12 years I was on that for 11  months  you know without a break and often  without a weekend the most satisfying  moment is walking into a theater with an  audience who have not seen the movie  before and hopefully are going to engage  with it and sitting in the middle of a  theater like this with people around who  don't know that you've worked on the  film and seeing the feeling the audience  react around you to that that's the most  satisfying thing
 

